stuff, why don't you

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.390
tell us a little
bit about yourself

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:47.650
and what you do here at Google.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:48.358
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:06:48.358 --> 00:06:50.902
Well, I've been at
Google a very long time.

00:06:50.902 --> 00:06:52.360
But most recently
I've been working

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:55.540
on the Kubernetes and Google
Container Engine project.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.850
I was one of the first
engineers on the project, one

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.100
of the original
four, I like to say.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:05.530
I work on things like networking
storage, API, node, federation,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:08.380
plug-ins, cloud provider,
those sorts of things, mostly

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.499
the lower level
parts of the system,

00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:12.790
the infrastructure for the
infrastructure, if you will.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:15.100
I'm also working on things
like governance and steering

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.400
committee, setting overall
direction for the project,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.320
both inside Google
with Container Engine

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:22.990
and with Kubernetes
proper, though I reserve

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.110
the right to hold opinions on
things that I don't necessarily

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.810
work on, which I think every
contributor to the project

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:30.820
is probably run into.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:32.800
I also do a lot of
outreach and evangelism.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.500
I go and talk to a lot
of customers and users

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.997
and would-be users
and conferences

00:07:36.997 --> 00:07:38.080
and those sorts of things.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I've seen
you on stage a couple of times.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.090
I've got to say, those talks
are really, really good.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:42.790
MARK MANDEL: Thank you.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:43.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So you mentioned

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.412
that you've been at Google
for a very long time.

00:07:45.412 --> 00:07:46.870
And now I'm curious--
for how long?

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.562
TIM HOCKIN: I joined in 2004,
so that puts me at 13 years.

00:07:49.562 --> 00:07:50.270
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK,
that is a very long time.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:52.750
MARK MANDEL: That's
a long time, nice.

00:07:52.915 --> 00:07:53.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:53.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:54.641
MARK MANDEL: That's super cool.

00:07:54.641 --> 00:07:56.650
So we've had several
episodes on Kubernetes.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.233
But I always love
asking this question

00:07:58.233 --> 00:07:59.800
because we get
different perspectives

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.910
on the project, which I
think are really interesting.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:03.310
So if you had to give sort
of like a two or three

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:04.910
minute description
of what Kubernetes

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.550
is if people haven't
come to it before,

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:08.210
how would you
describe Kubernetes?

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:10.210
TIM HOCKIN: Kubernetes
is, in a sentence,

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:13.150
it's a container
management system.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.220
I think the important
part here is

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:20.230
that it's a cluster management,
and it brings these new ideas--

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:22.000
or not new, but a
different idea--

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.090
of how people should
manage their workloads.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.250
It takes the lessons
that we've learned

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:30.970
by doing this for a long time
at Google and brings it out

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.730
and shows people
sort of how we work,

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:36.340
why we think it's a
successful way to work,

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.900
and brings forward
these primitives.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.380
So it's somewhat
opinionated, in that it's

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.260
got some basic ideas of how
we think you should do things.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.240
But it's not very
opinionated, in that

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.890
there's a lot of
flexibility, and you

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:51.400
can do things in many,
many ways within the bounds

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.020
of Kubernetes.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.880
And you can evolve your
operations over time.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:57.670
We bring this idea
that you should

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.610
manage your workloads
in aggregate instead of

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.260
in singletons.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.240
You should not be
thinking about which

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.970
particular instance of your
application is doing what.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:09.460
You should be thinking
about the number of replicas

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.850
you need and the scale
out, and the resources

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.880
should be allocated and managed
by the system automatically.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:17.980
These are not problems
that people, humans,

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.120
are good at thinking about.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.980
We should let the machines
deal with those problems.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:22.900
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.316
You mentioned
previously, as well,

00:09:24.316 --> 00:09:26.980
that you do some governance
and some aspects of sort

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:28.434
of the community.

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:30.225
It seems like the
community is a huge deal,

00:09:30.225 --> 00:09:31.660
like that the fact
that it exists

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.262
and how large it
is for Kubernetes.

00:09:33.262 --> 00:09:34.720
Can you talk a
little bit about it?

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:35.560
Is it still growing?

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.726
Like, what's happening with
the community right now?

00:09:37.726 --> 00:09:41.707
TIM HOCKIN: The community is
the single most important facet

00:09:41.707 --> 00:09:42.790
of the Kubernetes project.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.580
It is the reason that
Kubernetes is as successful

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:46.690
as it has been.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.740
The community is enormous.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.260
It's overwhelming, honestly.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:53.380
The last time I looked we
had over 2,500 contributors

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.390
across all of our
different repos,

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:58.390
coming from literally
hundreds of companies.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:02.620
We're pulling in somewhere
between 40 and 50 commits a day

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.200
to the code base.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.970
We're pushing our 50,000
commit number soon.

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:10.820
And it's incredible that we
have this many people working

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.800
on this little idea that we had.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.650
And the community has just
been such an integral part

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:16.790
of the process.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:19.880
It has simultaneously
forced us to be

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.074
more open with how we do
things, which has been

00:10:22.074 --> 00:10:23.240
really good for the project.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:26.060
But it's also forced
us to let go of parts

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:29.420
that we wanted to work on
or that historically we've

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:30.320
sort of owned.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.510
And we've let
community own things.

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:34.754
And it's been, I'd say,
overall wildly successful

00:10:34.754 --> 00:10:35.795
and incredibly difficult.

00:10:35.795 --> 00:10:37.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:39.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I'm wondering--

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:40.250
we mentioned community.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:42.290
How big is the community?

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:43.712
Like how many contributors?

00:10:43.712 --> 00:10:45.920
Could you tell everybody
how many people are actually

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:50.120
working on Kubernetes,
let alone using it,

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:52.640
but actually actively
developing it?

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:53.930
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.120
I mean the number of
total unique contributors

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.070
is over 2,500.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.320
That doesn't mean that 2,500
people are contributing

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.550
on a regular basis, right?

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.640
A lot of those are one-time
contributions or people

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.790
who contributed and then went
away to do something else.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.610
I don't have the number
off the top of my head.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.860
But I'd say it's
probably on the order

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:18.020
of several hundred 30-day
unique contributors from dozens

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:19.520
and dozens of companies.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.280
I mean, obviously, we have big
contributing companies Red Hat,

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:25.300
and we have many, many smaller
contributing companies.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.080
And we have a lot of
people who are just

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:28.640
starting to contribute.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.030
Our Slack is filled
with thousands of people

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:35.660
asking and answering questions
from each other all day long.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.670
Our mailing list is
incredibly useful.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.450
And people are there answering
questions for each other.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:43.280
We have thousands
of GitHub forks.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.660
We have hundreds of
meet-ups around the world.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:50.720
By most metrics, we're the
busiest project on GitHub.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.614
So, yeah, those are some
basic numbers for you.

00:11:53.614 --> 00:11:54.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.840
I like to think myself as
one of the contributors

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:58.965
because I fixed one typo
in the documentation once.

00:11:58.965 --> 00:12:00.006
TIM HOCKIN: There you go.

00:12:00.006 --> 00:12:00.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:02.120
You are one of those 2,500.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:02.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That counts.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:03.110
Yep.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:03.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:04.860
MARK MANDEL: I
better do something.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:05.870
I better get stuck in there.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:06.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.860
I got a cool T-shirt that
said contributor with a K.

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:09.140
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:09.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So there you go.

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:11.250
MARK MANDEL: There you go.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.333
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So let's
get started a little bit

00:12:13.333 --> 00:12:15.210
with more technical stuff.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:18.470
Kubernetes 1.7 has
a lot of new things.

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:20.330
I was looking at
the release notes,

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:24.350
and it's kind of amazing
and almost, like, scary

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:25.790
how many new things are coming.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.710
But why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:31.160
about what are the main topics
and your favorite features

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:31.940
maybe.

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:32.648
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:12:32.648 --> 00:12:35.210
I mean, the release notes
are overwhelming, right?

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.890
It's like 30 pages
of release notes.

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:37.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:38.340
TIM HOCKIN: You have
to remember Kubernetes

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.430
is an enormous
surface area, right?

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:43.940
Everything from network
infrastructure to the API

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:47.542
to the machinery that drives the
APIs to all the workloads, all

00:12:47.542 --> 00:12:50.000
of these things are being driven
by different teams, right?

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.360
We have over 100 people
at Google working on it,

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.340
and those people are
doing full-time work.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.310
So the rate of
change is just huge.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:12:59.810
It's not really
that surprising when

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:01.749
you look at the surface
area of it, right?

00:13:01.749 --> 00:13:04.040
It's when you sit down to
try to read the release notes

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.626
and ingest the whole thing at
once that it becomes scary.

00:13:07.626 --> 00:13:08.750
What are my favorite parts?

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:10.333
I mean, obviously,
I'm a little biased

00:13:10.333 --> 00:13:12.690
towards the parts
that I'm closest to.

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.960
And I'm close to them because
I'm interested in them.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.501
But some of the
things that I thought

00:13:16.501 --> 00:13:19.370
were really cool
in 1.7 release are

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:21.260
things like network
policy, which

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:25.640
went GA, which lets you sort
of describe micro-segmentation

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:26.730
on your network.

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:31.100
We have this ability to
preserve client IPs across load

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.410
balancers now,
which is something

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.240
that people asked
for a long time.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.144
We have this new
aggregated PI system,

00:13:37.144 --> 00:13:38.810
which I think is just
phenomenally cool.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:41.510
We've been talking about
it for literally two years

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:43.070
and working out
how to get it done.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.850
And now that it works, it's
actually pretty awesome.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:49.490
We have lots of new,
sort of, small features

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:51.860
that make the overall
distributedness of the system

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.300
better, things
like initializers,

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.610
which lets you do
out-of-process asynchronous

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:56.930
initialization of objects.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:00.620
We have audit logging
finally, and it's just

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.980
a super cool feature for you to
be able to see who changed what

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:05.750
and when about my cluster.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:10.060
We have the beginnings of
a much more secure system.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:11.930
We have encryption at rest.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.720
We have-- we're working
on moving all the cloud

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.136
providers out of our tree.

00:14:16.136 --> 00:14:18.260
I like to say I'm kicking
Google out of Kubernetes.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:18.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:19.418
MARK MANDEL: Hmm.

00:14:19.418 --> 00:14:21.702
TIM HOCKIN: And it's
a really big deal

00:14:21.702 --> 00:14:23.660
because it's going to
help the code base scale,

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.420
and it's going to allow
us to expand more broadly.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:28.130
We've got a new
metrics API, which

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:31.610
means we can finally start to
do things like auto-scaling

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.740
based on custom metrics.

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:37.010
The internal runtime
interface, the CRI,

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:38.870
we removed all of the
legacy Docker code.

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.400
It's now only CRI
Docker, which is

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.610
a phenomenal internal cleanup.

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:45.860
We have local storage
that's been introduced,

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:48.026
which is something that
people have been asking for,

00:14:48.026 --> 00:14:48.770
for a long time.

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:49.670
I mean, I could go on and on.

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:51.590
But these are some of
the things that jump out

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:52.550
to me about this release.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:54.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so there's
really nothing going on at all.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:54.880
Like, it's just been quiet.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:55.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:57.670
Yeah, Kubernetes
is really not --

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:58.250
I don't know.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:14:59.921
200 engineers, they're
not doing much.

00:14:59.921 --> 00:15:00.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:00.980
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it's--

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:01.370
I don't know.

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:01.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:02.593
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No.

00:15:02.593 --> 00:15:05.295
So instead of trying
to list all of them,

00:15:05.295 --> 00:15:07.420
because, as you mentioned,
there's so many of them,

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.253
I think that we have a
list of like the ones

00:15:09.253 --> 00:15:11.380
that we consider
to be the coolest.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:14.140
So why don't you go first
with some of your favorites.

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:15.140
And we asked Tim to--

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:16.390
MARK MANDEL: That sounds good.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:17.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --explain a
little bit why it's important.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:18.640
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so Tim actually

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:20.080
brought up one of my favorites.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.182
So it feels like
Kubernetes in the beginning

00:15:22.182 --> 00:15:23.890
was kind of like, we
think you were going

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:26.015
to solve sort of the 80%
rule, like this is what we

00:15:26.015 --> 00:15:27.589
think 80% of the people need.

00:15:27.589 --> 00:15:29.380
And then as we've headed
down the road map,

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:30.850
things have got more--

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:31.630
we still do that.

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.790
But we're now going to allow
you to have hooks and things you

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:35.357
can do to really
customize Kubernetes

00:15:35.357 --> 00:15:37.940
to be exactly what you need for
exactly what your workload is.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:41.110
So that brings me to things like
the API aggregation-type stuff

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:42.707
that you alluded to earlier.

00:15:42.707 --> 00:15:45.040
Can you sort of explain what
that is and how that works?

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:46.480
I think this is a really,
really cool thing.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:47.188
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:15:47.188 --> 00:15:49.570
So one of the things I think
that makes Kubernetes really

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:52.480
powerful is the consistent API.

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.620
We have one API server
that has a set of semantics

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:58.220
for how you interact with it.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:02.246
And all of our APIs conform
to that same API server.

00:16:02.246 --> 00:16:04.120
And they're all discoverable
in the same way,

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.640
and they're all sort of
interoperable in the same way.

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:09.430
And what that means is you
can have one set of tooling

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:13.060
and one sort of client
concept that works across all

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:14.080
of those different APIs.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:16.663
And like you said, it's sort of
the 80% rule, although I don't

00:16:16.663 --> 00:16:18.670
think we're quite at
80% yet, but the things

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:20.620
that we know how to
handle, sort of, natively

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.240
built into Kubernetes.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.880
And then there's this
wide landscape of ideas

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:27.322
that people have
of things either

00:16:27.322 --> 00:16:28.780
that they want to
add to Kubernetes

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:30.610
but they need to prove
themselves first,

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:32.830
or things that they don't
want to add to Kubernetes,

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:34.990
but they want to
use with Kubernetes

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:37.900
in the same way as Kubernetes
but probably will never

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:39.560
be part of the core system.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:42.270
And we've heard that
people who want to do this,

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:44.480
and what we've given them
is this aggregated API.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.390
And so what it means
is you can essentially

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:49.720
run your own program in your
cluster or out of your cluster.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:51.070
It doesn't really matter.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.610
The program will register
with the API server

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.370
as a specific sub API.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:00.040
And then you can use
all the existing tooling

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:01.020
that you've got--

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:04.690
Cube Control and all
of the other pieces.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.220
And they just work
with the new API,

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:08.349
as if that type that
you just registered

00:17:08.349 --> 00:17:10.210
was part of the built-in API.

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:13.030
When you post an HTTP
request to our API server,

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:16.030
it gets passed through
onto your own API server,

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:19.180
where you have a chance to
validate it, do admission

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:22.420
control, do sanity
checking of the input,

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:24.680
reject the request
if you need to.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:27.702
And if you accept it, then
it becomes a stored object

00:17:27.702 --> 00:17:30.160
in the same way that anything
else in the Kubernetes system

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:30.980
works.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.820
And it's essentially
transparent to the client.

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:36.250
And because something
like kubectl

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:39.070
does automatic probing
of the API server,

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:41.710
you can, in fact,
just start using it

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.030
without any changes
needed in your client.

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:46.690
MARK MANDEL: So has this got a
relationship to custom resource

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:47.367
definitions?

00:17:47.367 --> 00:17:47.950
Is that right?

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.140
TIM HOCKIN: Yeah, so
we started talking

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:54.310
about this aggregated API
idea a couple of years ago.

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:57.220
And when we scoped it out,
it was sort of big and hairy

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:58.360
and complicated.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.180
And so the predecessor to
custom resource definitions,

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.010
which is third-party
resources, was

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:05.770
sort of the poor man's
version of aggregated API.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.530
It was a simple way
to do essentially

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:09.820
the CRUD operations--

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:11.860
Create, Read,
Update, and Destroy.

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:13.660
But it didn't have the
opportunity for you

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:16.990
to hook in and do synchronous
validation, right?

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:19.450
You could never do a
sort of schematic check

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.570
on it to make sure that, say,
you have an integer field

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:24.111
and it has to be
greater than 10.

00:18:24.111 --> 00:18:26.110
There was no way for us
to validate that, right?

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:28.390
You had to do that
sort of after the fact.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.700
And that was great,
and it lets people

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:32.740
do a lot of really
neat prototyping stuff

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:34.160
really, really cheaply.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:35.920
But I think the
aggregated API gives you

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.040
a much more robust way to take
those ideas into production.

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:42.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So would
you say that custom resource

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.130
definitions is kind
of like a good way

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.860
to start if you want
to enhance the API,

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:49.570
but if you want to
get more serious you

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:51.250
should go with API aggregation?

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.110
Or should you just use API
aggregation rather than

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:55.727
custom resource definitions?

00:18:55.727 --> 00:18:57.310
TIM HOCKIN: I think
you said it right.

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:59.290
I think custom
resource definition

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:00.610
is the way you start.

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:03.490
It's the lowest barrier to
entry to making extensions

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:04.810
to the Kubernetes API.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.820
In fact, we request
anybody who's

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:10.060
trying to propose a
new Kubernetes API,

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:12.610
that you start with
something like a CRD

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:15.650
and you demonstrate the value
of what you're working on.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:18.820
You work out the API, and
you implement your back ends

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:21.629
against it, and you sort of
get everything sorted out.

00:19:21.629 --> 00:19:23.170
And then when that
looks pretty good,

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:26.470
then we can talk about
inclusion as a built-in API,

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:28.810
or we can talk about
moving it to something

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.640
like an aggregated API.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.520
Or you can leave that as a
CRD, if that's sufficient

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:36.130
for you and it satisfies
what you need to do.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.440
CRD is the lowest
barrier to entry.

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:41.080
Aggregated API is a
significantly higher barrier

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:44.574
to entry, but not nearly as high
as adding something built in.

00:19:44.574 --> 00:19:45.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:47.170
Let's move to the next topic.

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:49.750
I'm curious about this
thing because I've always

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:52.270
heard about it as
being something

00:19:52.270 --> 00:19:55.300
that people consider
an anti-pattern, which

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:58.210
is PetSets, or StatefulSets.

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:01.390
You are adding more things to
StatefulSets in this release.

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:03.317
Could you tell us a
little bit about what

00:20:03.317 --> 00:20:06.380
is, first, StatefulSets
and then when to use them?

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:07.110
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.000
Now, PetSet is sort
of a dirty word, so--

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:10.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I know.

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:11.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:16.250
TIM HOCKIN: So the idea stems
from this pets-cattle analogy

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:19.070
that has sort of made
its way around the space.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:24.470
And Kubernetes really embraces
the idea of cattle and scale

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:26.870
out and replicas.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:30.080
And that's really good
for a large class of work,

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.480
where you can just add
more copies of your thing

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.700
and it will scale.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.980
It doesn't really
satisfy the case of I

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:37.940
have a database, right?

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:39.648
I have something that's
really important,

00:20:39.648 --> 00:20:41.420
and I have to
protect that state.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:43.550
And this pattern sort
of is at the bottom

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:45.400
of every major
application, right?

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:46.610
It's the database.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:47.480
It's your Mongo.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:48.830
It's your My SQL.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:51.650
So once we sort of decided
we were successful enough

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:54.800
at stateless applications,
the scale-out web-serving sort

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:58.250
of applications, people wanted
to run stateful applications.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:20:59.960
They said, well, how
does Google do it?

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:03.530
And we said, well, you know,
the Google model within Borg

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:05.600
is a little different
than the Kubernetes model.

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:09.110
And we took a hard look at
what people were trying to do,

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:11.240
and we came up with
this idea of sort

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:14.570
of a different primitive that
has some of the same properties

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:17.780
as deployments or
replica sets but is

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:19.580
different in really
fundamental ways.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:22.070
And that was what became
what was then called PetSet,

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:23.660
which evolved into StatefulSet.

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:25.520
And some of the
differences are things

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:28.430
like you're not going to
scale it out in general.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.899
And if you do, it's not a
cavalier operation, right?

00:21:30.899 --> 00:21:32.690
You can't just throw
another replica at it.

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:35.270
It's typically something
where maybe you have a quorum,

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:37.700
and so you have to have
an odd number of replicas.

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:40.634
Or you need to do sort of
an introduction step, where

00:21:40.634 --> 00:21:42.050
you introduce a
new replica and it

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:43.760
meets all of the other
replicas, and then

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:44.850
you can add a second one.

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:47.110
So you can't add five
at a time, for example.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:47.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:47.757
MARK MANDEL:

00:21:48.256 --> 00:21:50.930
TIM HOCKIN: And the identity of
these replicas sort of matters,

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:51.430
right?

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:53.720
Whether you're shard one
or shard two actually

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:54.740
is significant.

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:56.450
And maybe you have
state that you're

00:21:56.450 --> 00:22:01.520
storing that is related to your
particular index in this array.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.190
And so these are the
those sort of primitives

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:05.180
that are added, right?

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:07.010
Within a replica
set or a deployment,

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:08.710
replicas are all
assumed fungible.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:09.770
You don't have an index.

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:11.520
You don't have an
ordinal number.

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:13.520
Nobody is the zeroth entry.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.500
And within a PetSet,
or a StatefulSet,

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:16.700
you absolutely do, right?

00:22:16.700 --> 00:22:18.620
You know exactly how
many replicas you have,

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:20.660
and you know which one you are.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:23.720
And it is tied to
volumes, so you

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:27.230
can have a volume that goes with
each copy of your StatefulSet

00:22:27.230 --> 00:22:28.380
application.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.130
And there's rules about
how upgrades are done

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:33.230
across StatefulSet replicas.

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:37.340
And we've taken the assumption
that there must be state here,

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:39.380
so be super, super careful.

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:42.290
And we've traded
instead of being really

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:44.180
fast at doing
rolling updates, it's

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:45.890
much more careful,
much more safe,

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.530
which means things are going to
be necessarily slower and more

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:49.500
complicated.

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:52.640
So now that said,
what's changed in 1.7

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:55.710
is we've extended it to support
things like rolling updates,

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:56.210
right?

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:57.680
So previously,
StatefulSet didn't even

00:22:57.680 --> 00:22:58.610
support rolling update.

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:00.318
If you wanted to update
your application,

00:23:00.318 --> 00:23:03.170
you had to manually go through
and remove each replica

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.312
and let each replica
get replaced.

00:23:05.312 --> 00:23:07.520
And so now we actually have
a rolling update ability,

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:10.260
where you can kick it, and
it will do that automatically

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:10.760
for you.

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:12.170
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:15.170
So Francesc and I have listed
both two of our favorite sort

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:16.180
of things in this list.

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:17.930
Tim, do you have a
particular favorite one

00:23:17.930 --> 00:23:19.400
you want to go deeper onto?

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:21.150
TIM HOCKIN: I mean, I
love network policy.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:22.820
I think it's a really cool API.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.860
It's been beta for a
while, but it's finally GA.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:28.580
Network policy lets
you sort of abstractly

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:30.730
describe the graph
of your application,

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:33.230
like which things are allowed
to talk to which other things.

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:34.630
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:36.170
TIM HOCKIN: And
then you can enforce

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:38.510
at the network level,
anything that's not described

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:40.370
by this graph is disallowed.

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:42.470
And so I can say,
like my front ends

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:44.000
can talk to my middle tier.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.070
And my middle tier can
talk to my database.

00:23:46.070 --> 00:23:48.440
My front ends can never
talk to my database.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:50.840
And my front ends can
never talk to each other

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:52.830
because that would be weird.

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:56.180
And once I've drawn
that graph, then

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:58.950
if I have the right sort
of network installation--

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:00.350
and it's an abstract API, right?

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:03.770
So if you have the right network
config, then you turn it on,

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:06.000
and the network config
will start enforcing it.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.060
And suddenly those packets
between the front end

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:10.010
and the database
just get dropped.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:12.590
They go away into the ether,
and there's nothing that

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:15.110
if your front end got
compromised somehow,

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:17.009
it cannot attack the database.

00:24:17.009 --> 00:24:18.300
MARK MANDEL: That's super cool.

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:19.091
I didn't know that.

00:24:19.091 --> 00:24:21.920
So as an interesting
aside then, is that

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:24.572
somehow related to what's going
on in, say, the Istio project?

00:24:24.572 --> 00:24:26.030
Or is this outside
of your purview?

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.850
TIM HOCKIN: No, this
is-- so network policy

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:33.080
is at the layer 3,
layer 4, IP plus port.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:35.120
Istio is at layer 7, right?

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:38.810
It's really aware
of identity and it's

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.470
using that as it's rules.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:43.910
They are very similar, and I
think they're complimentary.

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:46.580
The network policy can act
as a really nice backstop

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:50.240
behind Istio policies,
or layer 7 policies.

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:52.662
Istio's just a specific
implementation of that.

00:24:52.662 --> 00:24:54.120
So I think they
work well together.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:56.480
I think space is very
new, and we're still

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:59.000
trying to figure out exactly
what is going to happen there,

00:24:59.000 --> 00:24:59.916
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:59.916 --> 00:25:02.720
So we already have been
talking for over 20 minutes.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:03.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.070
So we're almost
running out of time.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:06.520
But before we do
that, I actually

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:08.630
would like to talk a little
bit about the future.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.090
So is there anything
on the roadmap

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:13.330
1.8 or later that
you'd like to mention

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:14.470
that you're excited about?

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:16.511
TIM HOCKIN: I don't want
to pick on any one thing

00:25:16.511 --> 00:25:17.595
from like the 1.8 roadmap.

00:25:17.595 --> 00:25:18.760
There's a bunch of features.

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:20.350
You can go look at
the features repo

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:22.900
and see what people
are working on for 1.8.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:25.090
I like to think a
little bit further out

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:27.670
and see what the major
trends within the system are.

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:31.090
I think extensions and
modularity is a major trend.

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:33.220
You've seen a lot of
work happening here

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:36.400
with CRDs, with aggregated
APIs, with plug-in systems,

00:25:36.400 --> 00:25:39.730
with things like Ingress, which
are sort of abstract APIs,

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:42.880
with cloud providers, with
now kube control plug-ins.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:44.590
You can actually do
client-side plug-ins.

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:47.327
I think that the directions with
Istio are really interesting.

00:25:47.327 --> 00:25:49.660
I think we don't really know
what we're doing there yet,

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.701
but I think that Istio
has demonstrated that this

00:25:51.701 --> 00:25:53.360
is a really cool space.

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:55.700
I think things like Service
Broker are really exciting.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:57.199
And I think that
that is going to be

00:25:57.199 --> 00:25:59.350
a big part of how
people consume services

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:01.180
and Kubernetes in the future.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:04.210
I'm looking forward to
better cloud support.

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:07.270
As more cloud support
Kubernetes more natively,

00:26:07.270 --> 00:26:09.940
we're getting better
primitives out of these clouds.

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:11.650
I know the team
here at Google Cloud

00:26:11.650 --> 00:26:14.920
is obviously working very hard
to make Google Cloud support

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:16.266
containers more directly.

00:26:16.266 --> 00:26:18.640
I think we're going to see
really cool things with easier

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:21.100
and richer installation
and configuration

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:24.490
of clusters, more
consistent hybrid stories,

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:26.500
so you can run clusters
in multiple locations

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:28.480
and sort of use the same tools.

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:31.800
Right now it's open source,
so it's sort of the Wild West,

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:33.384
and everybody's doing
their own thing.

00:26:33.384 --> 00:26:34.841
And I think we're
coming to a place

00:26:34.841 --> 00:26:36.760
where we can start to
get some convergence.

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:40.640
I think the local storage idea
is just scratching the surface.

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:43.780
I think we're going to see a
lot better system de-bugability

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:46.690
introspection, a lot
less reasons to SSH

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:49.540
into your machines, and a
lot better automatic tooling.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:51.790
These are the big trends
that I'm paying attention to.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:54.454
Security, obviously
we're getting better at.

00:26:54.454 --> 00:26:55.870
With the encryption
at rest, we'll

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:59.950
have more secrets and
off plug-ins and secret

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:02.260
plug-ins and
encryption providers

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:04.864
and automatic identity providers
and those sorts of things.

00:27:04.864 --> 00:27:05.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:07.452
Well, I have to ask a question.

00:27:07.452 --> 00:27:08.160
TIM HOCKIN: Yeah.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:11.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cube C-T-L,
cube control, or cube cuddle?

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.890
TIM HOCKIN: In my real
life, I say cube cuddle.

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:14.994
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:14.994 --> 00:27:15.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:27:16.015 --> 00:27:17.265
TIM HOCKIN: When I'm talking--

00:27:17.265 --> 00:27:20.800
when I'm talking
publicly, I sometimes

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:22.600
say cube control because
apparently that's

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:25.000
what most people
expect it to be.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.330
But I still think they're
wrong, and it's cube cuddle.

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:28.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:31.360
I was curious, because you
actually used both during the--

00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:31.790
MARK MANDEL: You did.

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:33.373
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--during the podcast.

00:27:33.373 --> 00:27:35.230
TIM HOCKIN: So, yes,
I caught myself.

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:38.737
I try to say cube control
when I'm presenting something.

00:27:38.737 --> 00:27:40.570
And I caught myself
saying cube cuddle once.

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:41.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think
cube cuddle is the way

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:42.790
to go anyway, So yeah.

00:27:42.790 --> 00:27:44.414
MARK MANDEL: I have
a twitch in my eye.

00:27:44.414 --> 00:27:44.920
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:47.080
It's probably going to go
away any minute now, I'm sure.

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:48.610
TIM HOCKIN: Well, I
mean, the alternative

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:49.985
that I proposed
