00:27:49.985 --> 00:27:53.500
didn't get a whole lot
of likes, was "kub ectl,"

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:54.490
which is obviously--

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:56.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:58.940
--which is in alignment
with ioctl, right?

00:27:58.940 --> 00:27:59.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:00.205
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, true.

00:28:00.205 --> 00:28:00.520
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:01.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:03.400
MARK MANDEL: "kub ectl," you
know, I could get behind that.

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:03.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:04.800
I could totally get behind that.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
need to make T-shirts.

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:07.390
MARK MANDEL: That's fantastic.

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:07.990
Cool.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:11.380
Well, like we could talk to
you for ages and ages and ages.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.810
But before we wrap up,
is there anything at all

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:15.370
that you want to
mention or plug?

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:16.940
Or is there a feature that
you just think's amazing

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:19.600
and we haven't managed to cover
it that you want to talk about

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:20.851
before we wrap up things here?

00:28:20.851 --> 00:28:22.725
TIM HOCKIN: I mean, I
think I've talked about

00:28:22.725 --> 00:28:24.130
all my favorite Pet features.

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:26.910
The thing I want to plug
most is the community.

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:30.280
I want people who are listening,
who maybe are using Kubernetes

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:31.960
ideas but aren't
really participating,

00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:33.250
to come and participate.

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:35.050
The water's warm.

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:36.700
Come in and file bugs, right?

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:37.739
Try things out.

00:28:37.739 --> 00:28:39.280
And every time
something doesn't work

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:41.410
the way you think it
should, every time you

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:44.000
have a little bit of
friction, file a bug.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:45.920
This is how I
became the top fug--

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:46.860
the top fug biler--

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:48.190
the bug filer--

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:49.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:50.932
--too, is by trying
the system out

00:28:50.932 --> 00:28:53.140
and trying to push the
boundaries of what the API can

00:28:53.140 --> 00:28:55.390
do and finding weird
places where things didn't

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:56.770
work the way I expected them.

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:58.810
And surprise is
never a good thing,

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:00.700
so I want everybody
who's using the system

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:03.070
to file a bug every
time you get surprised.

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:05.860
And come in, and if you've
got some Pet thing that you

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:07.750
want to see change,
like the code is

00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:09.250
daunting because
of the size of it,

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:12.640
but we'll help you find the
places where you need to tie in

00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:13.930
and scratch your own itches.

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:15.040
This is open source.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:17.140
It's been a wildly successful
open source project.

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:20.110
I think it's demonstrated the
real power of open source.

00:29:20.110 --> 00:29:22.990
I just want more people
to participate and engage.

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:26.020
We need help on things like
contributor experience.

00:29:26.020 --> 00:29:28.240
We need help on
things like tooling.

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:31.660
You don't have to be
a PhD systems engineer

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.040
to participate in
this community.

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:36.070
You can come in and help
us with our build systems,

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:40.060
with our shell scripts, with our
tools and test infrastructure,

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.120
and with our GitHub bots.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:44.680
All of these things, I think,
are relatively low barrier

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:45.520
to entry.

00:29:45.520 --> 00:29:47.485
Even docs, just
contributing to the docs

00:29:47.485 --> 00:29:49.570
is massively important.

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:51.802
Number one complaint that
we hear about Kubernetes

00:29:51.802 --> 00:29:54.010
over and over again is that
the docs could be better.

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:55.370
And they're getting better.

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:55.960
We need help.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:57.544
Many hands make light work.

00:29:57.544 --> 00:29:58.752
MARK MANDEL: That reminds me.

00:29:58.752 --> 00:30:00.820
I've had a patch that
I've had to do that I've

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:01.570
been wanting to put in--

00:30:01.570 --> 00:30:01.925
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You should do it.

00:30:01.925 --> 00:30:04.260
MARK MANDEL: --for like four
weeks now that I should do.

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:04.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:06.260
TIM HOCKIN: This
time next year, I'd

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:08.921
like the contributors number
to be 5,000 instead of 2,500.

00:30:08.921 --> 00:30:09.670
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:11.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
challenging, but OK.

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:11.900
MARK MANDEL: OK, so hold on.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:13.910
It is-- we're recording
