00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:46.130
to sit down and have a chat.

00:30:46.130 --> 00:30:48.290
Learned a whole bunch of
stuff about Kubernetes 1.7

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:49.970
I'm super excited
about this release.

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:51.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, this
was one of those interviews

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:53.060
that I really didn't
want to finish.

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:53.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:54.080
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:55.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So many questions,

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:56.440
and he's such a cool guy.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:58.550
I wanted to keep talking to him.

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:00.680
But anyway, I guess
it's time to go

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:03.350
with our Question of the
Week, which fortunately

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:04.410
is about the same topic.

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:04.850
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:07.550
So it seems like a fairly common
question in a lot of ways.

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:09.050
And I've definitely
come across this

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:12.080
before, where it's
like, OK, I have pods.

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:13.550
I have containers.

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:15.890
How much stuff do I put
inside my containers?

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:18.350
How much stuff do I
put inside my pods?

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:19.910
Is there this
magical silver bullet

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:23.570
for like a rule that I can
just apply for just giving me

00:31:23.570 --> 00:31:24.840
the best way to do this?

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:26.130
Francesc, tell me more.

00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:27.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The answer is no.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:28.220
There's no silver bullet.

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:28.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:30.919
Because that's the whole point
of a silver bullet, if they--

00:31:30.919 --> 00:31:31.760
or maybe they exist.

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:31.940
I don't know.

00:31:31.940 --> 00:31:33.010
MARK MANDEL: They
exist for werewolves.

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:33.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
But, yeah, whatever.

00:31:33.640 --> 00:31:34.520
Sure, OK.

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:35.180
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:38.570
Anyway, not really, but
there are some good practices

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:41.130
or a good way of
understanding what pods

00:31:41.130 --> 00:31:42.830
versus containers
are that might help

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:46.520
you guiding the decision
between putting everything

00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:48.290
in a pod or everything
in a container

00:31:48.290 --> 00:31:50.320
or whatever you want to do.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:52.400
And there's actually
a really good talk

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:54.670
by a very good
friend, Tim Hockin--

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:55.010
MARK MANDEL: Strangely that.

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:56.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --strangely.

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:57.649
We'll have a link
in the show notes.

00:31:57.649 --> 00:31:59.180
And it's called "Kubernetes--

00:31:59.180 --> 00:32:01.130
Understanding Pods
Versus Containers."

00:32:01.130 --> 00:32:02.660
And he uses a very
cool metaphor,

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:05.340
which is imagine that
you're thirsty, right?

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:07.430
And if you're thirsty,
I could give you

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:10.430
just a bunch of particles,
like electrons and neutrons

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:13.970
and protons, and then
you do it, right?

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:16.040
And it's like,
sure, but no, right?

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:16.790
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:32:16.790 --> 00:32:18.748
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Like,
that's not very helpful.

00:32:18.748 --> 00:32:20.300
That could be like,
well, do you have

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:22.820
all of these different
libraries and tools and things?

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:24.370
Then it just gives them to you.

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:24.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:32:24.810 --> 00:32:26.309
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You want them to be

00:32:26.309 --> 00:32:28.767
organized in some way
in order to be useful.

00:32:28.767 --> 00:32:31.100
So, yeah, you're getting a
drink, fundamental particles.

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:32.534
That's not a good idea.

00:32:32.534 --> 00:32:33.950
The second part
is like, well, I'm

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:37.270
just going to give you
like hydrogen and oxygen,

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:39.950
and just, like, you
figure it out, right?

00:32:39.950 --> 00:32:42.050
And that might be like
containers, right?

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:45.090
You have a container,
which is hydrogen,

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:47.690
which hydrogen, by the way, is
one proton, one neutron, one

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:48.740
electron.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:51.830
Oxygen is one proton, one
neutron, one electron.

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:54.762
So you have that,
and just, like, OK,

00:32:54.762 --> 00:32:55.970
still not good enough, right?

00:32:55.970 --> 00:32:59.570
So sometimes what a
container is, is not enough.

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:02.360
You want higher level
of organization,

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:04.760
and that's exactly when
you put them together,

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:07.820
and you're just like, oh,
what if I put together

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:11.019
the hydrogen and the oxygen
and put two hydrogens and one

00:33:11.019 --> 00:33:12.560
oxygen. Now, all of
a sudden, there's

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:15.380
two containers of hydrogen,
one container of oxygen,

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:17.360
and you have water,
which is a pod.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:18.110
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:33:18.110 --> 00:33:20.151
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that's
the whole idea, right?

00:33:20.151 --> 00:33:23.030
Like, hydrogen is a
useful thing by itself.

00:33:23.030 --> 00:33:25.970
But it is when you put it
together with another hydrogen

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:29.640
and oxygen that you get all that
useful thing, which is water.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.565
Imagine that you
want a sweet water.

00:33:31.565 --> 00:33:32.675
Sweet water is it called?

00:33:32.675 --> 00:33:33.320
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
sweet water, yeah.

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:33.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, whatever.

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:35.570
MARK MANDEL: Water with sugar.

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:36.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sugary water.

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:36.999
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:36.999 --> 00:33:39.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You could
put it together the same,

00:33:39.290 --> 00:33:40.250
in a single pod.

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:42.680
You could put the
sugar and the water.

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:46.850
But then the problem is that
the sweetness of the end product

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:47.820
is fixed, right?

00:33:47.820 --> 00:33:51.029
You cannot augment the amount
of water or sugar without

00:33:51.029 --> 00:33:52.070
augmenting the other one.

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:53.530
MARK MANDEL: And maybe
I find it too sweet.

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:54.446
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:54.446 --> 00:33:56.450
So the whole idea is
that, well, if you have it

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:58.310
in different pods,
all of a sudden

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:01.770
your cluster can scale
up the amount of water

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:03.380
or scale down the
amount of sugar

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:05.070
independently from each other.

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:06.320
And that's the whole idea.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:09.739
So you have containers, which
are like basic useful things--

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:12.469
hydrogen, oxygen.
Then you have pods,

00:34:12.469 --> 00:34:16.460
which are useful things, which
are like water and sugar.

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:18.800
And since they're
separated, you can actually

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:20.283
scale them differently.

00:34:20.283 --> 00:34:21.949
And I think that's a
beautiful metaphor.

00:34:21.949 --> 00:34:22.860
MARK MANDEL: I think
that works really well.

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:24.500
So basically, I
want to think about

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:26.350
what are the things that
need to be held together,

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:28.420
but what are the things that
I want to keep separate.

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:28.517
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:34:28.517 --> 00:34:29.690
MARK MANDEL: And making
sure those two rules

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:32.030
don't necessarily overlap in
ways that I don't mean them to.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:34.071
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If we go
to an example like with

00:34:34.071 --> 00:34:35.931
a three-layered tier whatever--

00:34:35.931 --> 00:34:37.639
MARK MANDEL: So I
might have my database.

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:38.929
I don't want that to
scale up and down.

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:40.512
I want that to stay
relatively static.

00:34:40.512 --> 00:34:41.880
So that's one that might--

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:43.940
a database process that
runs inside a container,

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:45.650
probably inside something
like a StatefulSet.

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:46.790
And I might run
just one of them.

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:48.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Or you
might have like a distributed

00:34:48.710 --> 00:34:50.389
one, where you have
like a cluster,

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:51.409
and you have like three of them.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:53.420
But they're like three,
and they're pretty stable--

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:53.710
MARK MANDEL: They're stable.

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:54.366
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --overall.

00:34:54.366 --> 00:34:55.080
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:56.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And then
you have the web servers.

00:34:56.510 --> 00:34:58.384
And the web servers
maybe have more of those.

00:34:58.384 --> 00:35:01.250
And maybe inside of every
single web server there's a pod.

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:03.744
And you have one thing
which is a micro-service

00:35:03.744 --> 00:35:04.410
to do something.

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:06.450
And then you the other
one, which is log.

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:07.950
Logging without
the micro-service

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:08.720
doesn't make any sense, right?

00:35:08.720 --> 00:35:09.300
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:11.716
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you put
them together inside of a pod.

00:35:11.716 --> 00:35:14.299
But if you have like customer
macro-service and content

00:35:14.299 --> 00:35:15.840
micro-service, they
may be different,

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:17.040
because you maybe
you need more traffic

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:19.642
into customers than the other
one that is add content.

00:35:19.642 --> 00:35:20.350
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:22.398
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that's a whole metaphor,

00:35:22.398 --> 00:35:23.065
the water-sugar.

00:35:23.065 --> 00:35:24.522
MARK MANDEL: And
by the same token,

00:35:24.522 --> 00:35:27.120
you wouldn't want to mix your
web stuff with your database,

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:29.370
because that would be like--
we don't want to scale up

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:31.540
the database at the same time
as we'd scale up our web stuff,

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:32.700
and keep that all separate.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:33.300
I like that.

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:33.905
That's a cool metaphor.

00:35:33.905 --> 00:35:34.207
I like it.

00:35:34.207 --> 00:35:35.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it's
a very, very good metaphor,

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.740
so I thought it was a really
good way of explaining it.

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:40.470
I do not think there's a
recording for this talk.

00:35:40.470 --> 00:35:42.970
But there's lots up there, so
we'll have a link in the show,

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:44.270
and so you can check them out.

00:35:44.270 --> 00:35:44.850
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:46.740
All right, Francesc,
before we wrap up

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:48.450
today, what are you up to?

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:49.284
Where are you going?

00:35:49.284 --> 00:35:50.325
Is there a Just for Funk?

00:35:50.325 --> 00:35:51.750
I'm sure there's
a Just for Funk.

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:53.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
there was a Just for Funk

00:35:53.730 --> 00:35:56.880
